+++
locale = "en"
+++
# Tensor public contract — PML1-U1 (production tensor surface).
#
# Plain values — NOT autograd-aware. This module owns tensor construction,
# dtype/shape/value-storage facts, validation, and element access. The
# autograd wrapper lives in gradus:gradient. A function that needs to be
# differentiable composes these ops inside a function annotated in
# gradus:gradient.
#
# Public import: import from "gradus:tensor" tensor
# Depends on: gradus:dtype (DType tag, U2), gradus:shape (bounded element
# product — construction validator, U3). DAG: tensor → dtype, tensor → shape.
# The U1 tensor → math edge is dropped at U4: math.fab now owns the pure
# operation families and imports tensor (math → tensor is the live edge;
# tensor never referenced math — the import was unused).
#
# --- Surface (U1) ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# dtype        — element type, tagged at runtime as dtype.DType (U2). U1
#                admitted F32; PML1-U2 moved the tag to src/dtype.fab
#                with the full lattice (F32/F16/I32/U8) and the dtype system
#                (cast/round/serialize + promotion/rejection tables +
#                non-finite rules). The tag lives in dtype.fab because enum
#                variants cannot be referenced across modules in library
#                context today (SEM001) — see dtype.fab compiler-lane note.
# shape        — runtime `list<int>` dimension vector. Static rank stays
#                a type-level fact at materialization sites (the consumer
#                pins it, see below).
# value storage — row-major flat `list<f32>` inside the Tensor genus.
# construction  — construct (flat data + shape) and fill (fill); both
#                validate fail-closed and throw a typed TensorError.
# validation    — every construction/access path validates with typed errors;
#                valid() is the non-failable consistency predicate.
# element access — get(indices) with rank / negative / bounds checks and
#                row-major flat offset computation.
#
# Materialization: a validated Tensor is turned into a typed compiler tensor
# by the consumer, which pins the static shape:
#
#     fixum tensor<f32, [2,2]> g ← t.data ↦ tensor<f32, [2,2]>
#
# The `↦` conversion itself enforces the element count at runtime; valid()
# has already checked it, so a materialized tensor is consistent.
#
# --- Shape posture flag for PML1-U3 (hard shape assumption) ---------------
#
# U1 carries shape as a runtime dimension list in a staged Tensor genus —
# the "staged" option of the U3 generic/generated/staged posture decision.
# Alternatives were probed against the live compiler on 2026-08-08:
#   * generic-shape genus `genus Tensor<magnitudo F>` — applying a type
#     argument `Tensor<[2,2]>` is PARSE001 today;
#   * `tensor<f32, _>` (shape-hole) genus field fed by runtime-shape
#     construction (`seed.strue/crea` with a shape parameter) is
#     SEM014 cannot_infer_tensor_shape in standalone library context (no
#     call site to unify the hole);
#   * `tensor<f32, _>` return types from runtime-shape construction — same
#     SEM014 in library context; holes unify only when a call site pins the
#     shape.
# The staged carrier compiles today in library context and leaves the
# generic/generated choice open for U3. U3 also owns the broadcast/reshape/
# expand rules and the R3 one-row/one-shape non-hardcoding constraint.
#
# --- PML0 proof-helper ledger dispositions (attached, not re-decided) -----
#
# pml0-proof-api-ledger.md (gradus 33df687) dispositions, attached per row:
#   * admit (13, caller-backed) — nn 6: linear_2x2, linear_4x4, gelu_4x4,
#     linear_2x8, layernorm_2x8, gelu_2x8; loss 3: mse_2x2, mse_4x4,
#     mse_2x8; attention 1: scaled_dot_product_2x8; transformer 1:
#     bert_tiny_block_2x8; train 2: train_step_2x2, train_step_4x4. These
#     stay caller-backed until PML1-U6 executes the disposition.
#   * retire (4) — optimize 2: sgd_step_2x2, sgd_step_4x4; transformer 2:
#     attention_block_2x8, ffn_block_2x8 (no live caller; their math is
#     inlined in caller-backed functions). This surface re-admits none of
#     them.
# Per-row dispositions are executed at PML1-U6 (grep-verified removal or
# aliasing); U1 only attaches the ledger.
#
# --- Compiler boundary notes (probed 2026-08-08) --------------------------
#
#   * The gradus-ml-foundation finding "genus wrapping of AIR tensor types
#     is unsupported (PARSE001)" is superseded: `genus X { tensor<f32, [2,2]> data }`
#     now compiles. Shape-hole `tensor<f32, _>` fields compile only when fed
#     by static shapes (see U3 flag above).
#   * Failable propagation: `⇥ E` with a non-text E does not auto-
#     propagate compiler conversion failures (v1 channels are text/ignotum);
#     U1 wraps compiler-level fallible steps in fac/cape and re-throws
#     typed errors. `assert` is fatal and not cape-able by design.
#   * FMIR stepper: library imports execute via `faber run -t fmir`, but
#     genus construction/projection (missing metadata) and const-with-cast
#     module initializers (math.fab epsilon, transplant error) are not yet
#     supported by the stepper image. Runtime proof for library code is
#     therefore compile-level via `faber check` until the stepper catches
#     up. These are compiler-lane residuals, not Gradus workarounds.
#
# --- Seams ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   * dtype system (cast/round/serialize, non-finite, promotion tables) — U2,
#     landed in src/dtype.fab; the tag moved there (compiler-lane
#     finding: cross-module enum variant references unsupported).
#   * broadcast/reshape/expand rules + error identity — U3.
#     Implemented in gradus:shape (PML1-U3): broadcast / reshape /
#     expand with typed ShapeError; staged-carrier posture decision and
#     the R1-recheck compiler evidence are recorded in src/shape.fab.
#   * pure operation families (elementwise/reduce/matmul/cast/concat) — U4,
#     landed in src/math.fab.
#   * parameter identity/traversal (consumes this surface) — U5.
#   * serialization (versioned bytes, round-trip) — U7.
#   * runtime numeric proof (co-located .proba execution) — via the
#     cargo-backed `faber test` harness; auditor-owned per Cargo discipline.

import from "gradus:dtype" dtype

import from "gradus:shape" shape

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TensorError — typed error vocabulary of the production tensor surface.
# Every variant carries a text message so consumers can render the failure
# without matching each variant.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

@ public { }
union TensorError {
    InvalidShape {
        string message
    },
    ElementMismatch {
        string message
    },
    IndexOutOfBounds {
        string message
    }
}

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tensor — staged value carrier: dtype + runtime shape + row-major flat
# storage. Construction and access validate fail-closed. Value semantics:
# assignment moves; there is no borrow syntax in Faber (values are moved or
# cloned), matching the JAX-shaped model of the campaign.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------


@ public { }
class Tensor {
    dtype.DType dtype
    list<int> shape
    list<f32> data

    # Runtime shape (dimension vector). Nominal projection.
    fn shape() → list<int> {
        return self.shape
    }

    # Rank (number of dimensions).
    fn rank() → int {
        return self.shape.length()
    }

    # Element count (product of dimension sizes; [] → 1).
    fn numel() → int {
        var int product ← 1
        for from self.shape const dim {
            product ← product * dim
        }
        return product
    }

    # Dtype tag.
    fn dtype() → dtype.DType {
        return self.dtype
    }

    # Consistency predicate: flat storage length equals the shape product
    # and every dimension is non-negative. Non-failable by design.
    fn valid() → bool {
        if self.data.length() ≠ self.numel() then return false
        for from self.shape const dim {
            if dim ≺ 0 then return false
        }
        return true
    }

    # Element access — validated, typed errors. Rank must match the shape;
    # each index must be non-negative and strictly inside its dimension.
    # Flat offset is computed row-major (stride walk).
    fn get(list<int> indices) → f32 ⇥ TensorError {
        require self.numel() ≠ 0 throw variant IndexOutOfBounds {message = "empty tensor"}
        require indices.length() ≡ self.shape.length() throw variant IndexOutOfBounds {message = "index rank mismatch"}
        var int flat ← 0
        var int stride ← self.numel()
        var int i ← 0
        for from indices const idx {
            const int dim ← self.shape.get(i) coalesce 1
            require dim ≥ 0 throw variant InvalidShape {message = "negative dimension"}
            stride ← stride / dim
            require idx ≥ 0 throw variant IndexOutOfBounds {message = "negative index"}
            require idx ≺ dim throw variant IndexOutOfBounds {message = "index out of bounds"}
            flat ← flat + idx * stride
            i ← i + 1
        }
        return self.data.get(flat) coalesce 0.0
    }
}

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Error rendering — every TensorError variant carries a text message;
# message(e) exposes it so consumers can report failures without matching
# each variant.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

@ public { }
fn message(TensorError e) → string {
    match e {
        case InvalidShape const message {
            return message
        }
        case ElementMismatch const message {
            return message
        }
        case IndexOutOfBounds const message {
            return message
        }
    }
}

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Construction — free constructors (AGENTS.md: free functions for
# constructors). Both validate fail-closed and return typed errors.
#
# CTO-2 shape-policy (PML1 closeout): construction consumes ONE validator —
# the element product is routed through shape.numel (the bounded
# product), which checks negative dims and the element-count ceiling before
# any allocation sized by the count. ShapeError is mapped to TensorError
# preserving the message text.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

@ private { }
fn _numel_shape(list<int> shape) → int ⇥ TensorError {
    do {
        return shape.numel(shape)
    }
    catch err {
        const string c ← shape.message(err)
        throw variant InvalidShape {message = c}
    }
}

# Zero-shape Default for `var Tensor` bindings (varia on structs). Empty
# rank-0 carrier: F32 tag, [] shape, empty data. Not a validated value
# (`valid()` is false — numel of [] is 1); only a placeholder the caller
# overwrites, or returns from an unreachable cape arm.
@ public { }
fn default() → Tensor {
    return Tensor {dtype = dtype.f32(), shape = [], data = []}
}

# Build a Tensor from a flat row-major data list and a runtime shape.
# Rejects negative dimensions and element-count mismatch.
@ public { }
fn construct(list<f32> data, list<int> shape) → Tensor ⇥ TensorError {
    return construct_dtype(data, shape, dtype.f32())
}

# Build a Tensor from flat data + shape + an explicit dtype tag. Same
# fail-closed validation as construct; the dtype seam (PML1-U2) lets the
# caller tag the tensor's value domain. Value-domain enforcement (that
# data is representable in the tag) is the dtype module's cast contract.
@ public { }
fn construct_dtype(list<f32> data, list<int> shape, dtype.DType dtype) → Tensor ⇥ TensorError {
    const int product ← _numel_shape(shape)
    require data.length() ≡ product throw variant ElementMismatch {message = "element count mismatch"}
    return Tensor {dtype = dtype, shape = shape, data = data}
}

# Build a Tensor filled with one value over a runtime shape.
# Rejects negative dimensions.
@ public { }
fn fill(list<int> shape, f32 payload) → Tensor ⇥ TensorError {
    const int product ← _numel_shape(shape)
    var list<f32> data ← vacua
    for range 0‥product const i {
        data.append(payload)
    }
    return Tensor {dtype = dtype.f32(), shape = shape, data = data}
}
